  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday through Friday)\nIssued at 140 AM CDT Sat Oct 10 2026\n\nAforementioned storm systems still look to bring about more \nchangeable weather, including the return of rain/storm chances, \nthough focused areas and timing remain rather uncertain at this \npoint.\n\nForecast confidence on Tuesday is reasonably high, with a lead wave \nperturbation expected to instigate scattered afternoon/evening \nshowers and storms in a corridor stretching from the central Texas \nPanhandle into portions of western and northern Oklahoma. Provided \nincreasing mid and upper-level flow overspreading an unseasonably \nmoist/unstable boundary layer, potential will exist for strong-\nsevere storms during this time as well.\n\nConfidence regarding specifics on timing/amounts/severity of \nprecipitation drops dramatically by Wednesday. The primary \nuncertainty remains how a rather complex upper air pattern evolves\nby this time. Even ensemble guidance/systems are currently rather\ndivergent in their solutions, with the grand ensemble showing a \nmore progressive upper pattern compared to a clustered ensemble \nmean (i.e., taking consensus from three primary ensemble systems).\n\nWhat does this mean for you? While precipitation chances and \neventually cooler/more seasonable temperatures are likely in some \ncapacity across the region later next week, forecast specifics \n(i.e., temperatures, % chance and amount of rain, etc.) may change\nnotably over coming updates, so make sure to check back as a more\nunified solution begins to emerge.\n\nUngar",
